              "text": "That annoying whitish film on your glass is a very common phenomenon. If you are using the same glass for about a week, the film is almost certainly caused by one of two culprits (or a combination of both): **mineral deposits** or a **microbial biofilm**. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what these are, why they happen even with filtered water, and how to tell them apart.\n\n---\n\n### Culprit 1: Mineral Deposits (Limescale)\nYou might think that because your water is filtered, it shouldn\u2019t leave any residue behind. However, most standard refrigerator filters use **activated carbon**. \n* **What the filter does:** It removes chlorine, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), bad tastes, and odors.\n* **What the filter *doesn't* do:** It does **not** remove dissolved minerals like calcium and magnesium (which make water \"hard\"). Only specialized reverse osmosis (RO) systems or water softeners remove these.\n\nAs you drink from the glass, water coats the sides and evaporates. Over the course of a week, this continuous evaporation leaves behind tiny amounts of dissolved calcium and magnesium. Eventually, they build up into a visible, chalky white film of **calcium carbonate**.\n\n### Culprit 2: A Microbial Biofilm\nIf you are refilling and drinking from the same glass for a week without washing it, you are likely looking at a **biofilm**. \n* **How it forms:** Every time you take a sip, you introduce \"backwash\"\u2014microscopic food particles, saliva, and mouth bacteria\u2014into the glass. Additionally, dust, yeast, and mold spores naturally floating in the air land in the water. \n* **The Filter Paradox:** Because your refrigerator filter is highly effective at removing **chlorine** (the municipal disinfectant used to keep bacteria at bay), the water in your glass has no defense against microbial growth. \n* Over a few days, these bacteria and yeasts colonize the surface of the glass, creating a thin, slimy, translucent-to-white film. \n\n---\n\n### How to Tell Which One It Is\n\n* **The Texture Test:** \n  * If the film feels **rough, dry, or chalky**, it is likely mineral deposits.\n  * If the film feels **slippery, slimy, or greasy** when wet, it is a biofilm.\n* **The Vinegar Test:**\n  * Try wiping the film with a cloth dipped in white vinegar. If the film dissolves instantly, it is calcium (minerals). Acid breaks down calcium carbonate. \n  * If it doesn't budge with vinegar alone but easily wipes away with dish soap and warm water, it is a biofilm.\n\n---\n\n### How to Clean and Prevent It\n\n1. **Wash your glass more often:** From a hygiene perspective, you should swap out or wash your drinking glass every **1 to 2 days**. Letting a glass sit for a week allows bacteria to multiply to levels that could eventually make you feel unwell or make the water taste stale.\n2. **Dissolve mineral buildup:** If you have stubborn white spots or film that standard dishwashing won't remove, soak the glass in a mixture of **50% warm water and 50% white vinegar** for 15 minutes, then scrub gently with a sponge. \n3. **Sanitize:** To thoroughly kill any biofilm bacteria, wash the glass in a dishwasher or use hot water and antibacterial dish soap.",
